        "f": "<b>PV = C / r</b> &nbsp;(on the exam formula sheet)<br><br>C is the constant cash flow, r the discount rate matching its risk.<br><br><b>The date.</b> C/r is always the value <b>one period before the first payment</b>. It is a date-0 value <i>only</i> when the first C arrives at date 1.<br><br><b>First payment at date 3.</b> Then C/r is a <b>date-2</b> value, with two periods still to go:<br>PV<sub>0</sub> = (C/r) / (1 + r)<sup>2</sup><br><br><b>Why it matters.</b> This convention costs more points than any piece of algebra in the course. It is the error the solution keys call out when candidates use 100 rather than 100 × 1.03 as a certainty equivalent: the cash flow was taken from the wrong date. Draw the timeline before you write the formula.",
