        "s": "Write the free cash flow formula as it appears on the exam sheet, and explain each term in one line.",
        "f": "<b>FCF<sub>t</sub> = EBIT<sub>t</sub>(1 − τ<sub>c</sub>) + Depreciation<sub>t</sub> − CapEx<sub>t</sub> − ΔNWC<sub>t</sub> + Other<sub>t</sub></b><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>EBIT</td><td>Earnings before interest and taxes — operating profit, after depreciation</td></tr><tr><td>τ<sub>c</sub></td><td>Corporate tax rate, applied to <b>full EBIT</b>, as if the firm had no debt</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Added back: it was deducted for tax but nobody was paid</td></tr><tr><td>CapEx</td><td>The full cash cost of long-lived assets, in the year it is paid</td></tr><tr><td>ΔNWC</td><td>The <b>change</b> in net working capital, NWC<sub>t</sub> − NWC<sub>t−1</sub></td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>One-off after-tax items the problem gives you, e.g. proceeds from selling a machine</td></tr></table><b>Read it as four moves:</b> tax operating profit as if unlevered, add back the non-cash charge, subtract the cash spent on assets, subtract the cash tied up in the balance sheet.<br><br><b>EBIT(1 − τ<sub>c</sub>) is called unlevered net income.</b> Interest never appears anywhere in this formula — that is deliberate, and it is what makes the WACC and APV methods comparable.",
