        "s": "Define net working capital and the change in net working capital. Which items enter with which sign?",
        "f": "<b>NWC = cash required for operations + accounts receivable + inventory − accounts payable</b><br><b>ΔNWC<sub>t</sub> = NWC<sub>t</sub> − NWC<sub>t−1</sub></b><br><br><b>Why those signs.</b> Receivables and inventory are cash you have already spent and not yet got back, so they are money locked up: positive. Payables are the reverse — goods you hold but have not paid for, so the supplier is financing you: negative.<br><br><b>The formula subtracts the change, not the level.</b> Two errors are fatal to a chained answer:<br>(1) subtracting NWC<sub>t</sub> itself instead of the difference;<br>(2) reading \"working capital falls by 20.8\" and subtracting 20.8 — it is ΔNWC = −20.8, and −(−20.8) = <b>+20.8</b>.<br><br>Write the level in a row of its own, difference it, then feed the difference in. Never do it in your head.",
