        "f": "<b>r<sub>E</sub> = r<sub>U</sub> + (D/E)(r<sub>U</sub> − r<sub>D</sub>)</b> &nbsp;— <b>MM Proposition II</b>, and it <b>is</b> on the exam sheet.<br><b>β<sub>E</sub> = β<sub>U</sub> + (D/E)(β<sub>U</sub> − β<sub>D</sub>)</b> &nbsp;— the same statement in betas, not on the sheet.<br><br>Both come from rearranging the weighted average r<sub>U</sub> = (E/V)r<sub>E</sub> + (D/V)r<sub>D</sub>.<br><br><b>Which to use.</b> Whichever the question hands you inputs for. Given betas, use the beta line and convert once at the end; given rates, use the rate line. <b>Never convert twice</b> — each conversion is a chance to lose a decimal.<br><br><b>Note the D/E, not D/V.</b> If the problem gives you D/V = 0.35, then D/E = 0.35/0.65 = 0.5385. Mixing the two is one of the most common slips in this material.",
