        "s": "Define the recovery rate, the loss given default, the credit spread and the expected return on debt. Which of these is not on the formula sheet?",
        "f": "<b>R</b> — the <b>recovery rate</b>: the fraction of the promised payment creditors actually collect in default.<br><b>L = 1 − R</b> — the <b>loss given default</b>.<br><b>p</b> — the probability of default over the period.<br><b>Credit spread</b> = y − r<sub>f</sub>, the whole gap between the promised yield and the risk-free rate.<br><br><b>Expected payment:</b> E[payment] = (1 − p)F + p·R·F = <b>F·(1 − p·L)</b><br><br><b>Expected return on debt:</b> <b>r<sub>D</sub> = y − p·L</b><br><br><b>This last formula is NOT on the exam sheet. Memorise it.</b> r<sub>D</sub> is at most y, with equality only when p = 0 or L = 0.<br><br><b>The spread splits into two economically different pieces</b>, and only one is a risk premium:<br>• r<sub>f</sub> → r<sub>D</sub> is the <b>risk premium on debt</b>, priced by the CAPM: r<sub>D</sub> − r<sub>f</sub> = β<sub>D</sub>(E[R<sub>mkt</sub>] − r<sub>f</sub>).<br>• r<sub>D</sub> → y is the <b>expected loss</b> p·L, which compensates for nothing — it is padding so that the average payment comes out right.<br><br>Never compute β<sub>D</sub> from the yield. Get r<sub>D</sub> first, then the beta.",
